GHOST_GAINS — remove two costs from the accelerated SHA-256 Transform: the byte-swap libcall and the
per-block staging copy.

⛔ EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, AND IT MOVES METHOD_ID. Applied only when HAZYNC_SHA_FASTPATH=1.
⚠ Applies ON TOP of patches/0002, which is what introduces the Transform this edits.

Neither change alters a single hash. Both are plumbing that only became visible once the
cryptography stopped dominating.

1. __builtin_bswap32 -> inline shifts. rv32im has no byte-reverse instruction -- that is the Zbb
   extension -- so the builtin lowers to a LIBCALL to __bswapsi2, and the call frame costs more than
   the four shifts it performs. A profile of the current stack puts __bswapsi2 at 78 M cycles in the
   chunk work and 256 M in the aggregate: 334 M spent on byte order between two little-endian ends.

2. Skip the 64-byte staging copy when the chunk is already 4-byte aligned. That copy runs PER BLOCK
   and exists only to guarantee alignment for the uint32_t* the accelerator takes. memcpy is the
   largest single line in the aggregate profile at 656 M, with CSHA256::Write as its dominant
   caller. Core's internal buffers are aligned, so the common path takes no copy at all; the
   unaligned path is unchanged.

⚠ The alignment test is a real test, not an assumption -- an unaligned chunk still takes the copy.
Passing an unaligned pointer to the accelerator would be undefined behaviour, and this tree already
learned that guest C++ builds with -w and UB ships silently.

--- a/src/crypto/sha256.cpp
+++ b/src/crypto/sha256.cpp
@@ -103,18 +103,51 @@
  *  RISC0 stores state big-endian (SHA256_INIT uses .to_be) and takes the raw message bytes as the
  *  block; Core keeps native-endian state and reads the chunk big-endian, so we byte-swap the state
  *  in/out and pass the raw 64-byte chunk. */
+#ifdef HAZYNC_SHA_FASTPATH
+/* Inline byte reverse. Deliberately NOT __builtin_bswap32: without Zbb that lowers to a call to
+ * __bswapsi2, and the call dominates the work. Same value, no libcall. */
+#define HZ_BSWAP32(x) ((uint32_t)((((uint32_t)(x)) >> 24) | ((((uint32_t)(x)) >> 8) & 0x0000FF00u) \
+                                | ((((uint32_t)(x)) << 8) & 0x00FF0000u) | (((uint32_t)(x)) << 24)))
+#endif
 extern "C" void sys_sha_compress(uint32_t*, const uint32_t*, const uint32_t*, const uint32_t*);
 void Transform(uint32_t* s, const unsigned char* chunk, size_t blocks)
 {
     uint32_t st[8];
+#ifdef HAZYNC_SHA_FASTPATH
+    /* rv32im has no byte-reverse instruction (that is Zbb), so __builtin_bswap32 lowers to a LIBCALL
+     * to __bswapsi2 and the call frame costs more than the four shifts it performs. A profile of the
+     * current stack puts __bswapsi2 at 78 M cycles in the chunk work and 256 M in the aggregate --
+     * 334 M total, on byte order, between two little-endian ends. Written as shifts it inlines. */
+    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) st[i] = HZ_BSWAP32(s[i]);
+#else
     for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) st[i] = __builtin_bswap32(s[i]);
+#endif
     uint32_t blk[16];
     while (blocks--) {
+#ifdef HAZYNC_SHA_FASTPATH
+        /* The 64-byte staging copy runs PER BLOCK, and memcpy is the largest single line in the
+         * aggregate profile (656 M) with CSHA256::Write as its dominant caller. The copy exists only
+         * to guarantee 4-byte alignment for the uint32_t* the accelerator takes; when `chunk` is
+         * already aligned -- which Core's internal buffers are -- it can be passed straight through.
+         * Byte content is identical either way, so this changes no hash. */
+        if ((((uintptr_t)chunk) & 3u) == 0u) {
+            const uint32_t* w = (const uint32_t*)(const void*)chunk;
+            sys_sha_compress(st, st, w, w + 8);
+        } else {
+            __builtin_memcpy(blk, chunk, 64);
+            sys_sha_compress(st, st, blk, blk + 8);
+        }
+#else
         __builtin_memcpy(blk, chunk, 64);
         sys_sha_compress(st, st, blk, blk + 8);
+#endif
         chunk += 64;
     }
+#ifdef HAZYNC_SHA_FASTPATH
+    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) s[i] = HZ_BSWAP32(st[i]);
+#else
     for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) s[i] = __builtin_bswap32(st[i]);
+#endif
 }
 
 void TransformD64(unsigned char* out, const unsigned char* in)
